      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Following a spinal cord injury, patients are often burdened by chronic pain. Preliminary research points to activation of the motor cortex through increased mobility as a potential means of alleviating postinjury chronic pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to assess the relationship between pain severity and mobility among patients who have sustained a traumatic spinal cord injury while controlling for clinically-relevant covariates."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A multi-center, cross-sectional study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "The SCIMS is composed of 14 centers, all located in the United States and funded by the National Institute on Disability and Rehabilitation Research (NIDRR)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "POPULATION",
          "content": "The study cohort included 1980 patients who completed the one-year SCIMS follow-up assessment between October 2000- December 2013."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A multi-center, cross-sectional study was performed to assess the impact of mobility on self-reported pain using information from 1980 subjects who sustained a traumatic spinal cord injury and completed a year-one follow-up interview between October 2000 and December 2013. Patient information was acquired using the Spinal Cord Injury National Database, compiled by the affiliated Spinal Cord Injury Model Systems. Analyses included a multivariable linear regression of patients' self-reported pain scores on mobility, quantified using the CHART-SF mobility total score, and other clinically relevant covariates."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "After controlling for potential confounders, a significant quadratic relationship between mobility and patients' self-reported pain was observed (P=0.016). Furthermore, female gender, \"unemployed\" occupational status, paraplegia, and the presence of depressive symptoms were associated with significantly higher pain scores (P<0.02 for all variables). Statistically significant quadratic associations between pain scores and age at injury, life satisfaction total score, and the CHART-SF occupational total subscale were also observed (P≤0.03 for all variables)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Among patients with moderate to high levels of mobility, pain scores decreased with increasing mobility."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CLINICAL REHABILITATION IMPACT",
          "content": "Enhancing a patient's physical activity by increasing his or her mobility may reduce neuropathic pain if begun shortly after a spinal cord injury."
        }
      ]},
